      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "First ionisation energy is the energy needed to remove the outermost electron from each atom in a mole of gaseous atoms. A mole is a fixed, very large number of atoms. Gaseous just means the atoms are separate, with nothing else attached. For sodium, each atom loses one electron and becomes a sodium ion. That takes four hundred and ninety-six kilojoules for a mole of sodium atoms. It's called first, because it removes only the first, most loosely held electron.",
