      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "Now go across Period three. From sodium to argon, the first ionisation energy generally rises, from four hundred and ninety-six, to fifteen hundred and twenty-one kilojoules per mole. That's the same cause as last time. The core charge climbs while the electrons stay in the same shell, so the atoms get smaller, and the outer electrons are held more tightly. It takes more energy to pull one off. Removing chlorine's outer electron takes about two and a half times the energy needed for sodium's.",
