        }
      ],
      "requestAttempts": 0,
      "pronunciationText": "So both measures rise across a period and fall down a group, but they're not the same thing. Ionisation energy is an energy, for one separate atom losing an electron. Electronegativity is a number on a scale, for an atom in a bond pulling on a shared pair. The noble gases show the difference. Argon has the highest first ionisation energy in Period three. But noble gases almost never form bonds, so they usually have no electronegativity value at all. That doesn't mean zero. It means there's nothing to measure.",
